Thyroglossal Duct Cyst
Opthalmology & ENT
Midline neck mass, Elevates with swallowing, Elevates with tongue protrusion

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-044
Reason for Visit: Sudden, painful enlargement and severe redness of a previously unnoticed neck lump in a young patient.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a rapidly expanding, acutely painful, and erythematous midline neck mass in a child/young adult, accompanied by mild dysphagia. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to evaluate for impending airway compromise, differentiate an infected thyroglossal cyst from a rapidly expanding deep neck space abscess or acute thyroiditis, and to initiate immediate targeted antibiotic therapy prior to routine clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a lump right in the middle of my throat that blew up today. It is bright red, hot, and hurts every time I swallow."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a previously painless, asymptomatic, but palpable midline neck mass located strictly at the level of the hyoid bone, noticed incidentally months ago with a slow, insidious growth pattern. Over the last 24 hours, the cyst became acutely infected, demonstrating sudden localized tenderness, rapid enlargement, and overlying erythema. Endorses mild dysphagia and a sensation of anterior neck fullness. Denies stridor, severe shortness of breath, voice changes, or systemic endocrine symptoms (palpitations, heat intolerance).
Functional Status: Uncomfortable, guarding the neck, avoiding swallowing large boluses.
Social/Safety Concerns: Parent highly anxious regarding a throat abscess or thyroid emergency.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 92 | Temp: 37.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 55 Kg
General: Uncomfortable but not toxic-appearing. No stridor or respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Inspection reveals a 3cm, markedly erythematous, tender, and fluctuant midline anterior neck mass located at the level of the hyoid bone. Palpation demonstrates a firm, well-circumscribed nature beneath the acute edema. Crucially, there is distinct, visible upward movement of the cystic mass upon voluntary swallowing, and characteristic upward movement explicitly during tongue protrusion. The normal thyroid gland is palpable in its correct orthotopic anatomical position lower in the neck. Airway is patent, no tracheal deviation.
Environmental Observations: Standard living environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acutely Infected Thyroglossal Duct Cyst - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Branchial Cleft Cyst - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The anatomical location being strictly midline rather than lateral (anterior to the sternocleidomastoid), and the mass elevating specifically with tongue protrusion.

Acute Thyroiditis/Thyroid Nodule - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The mass moving distinctly with tongue protrusion (a pathognomonic sign linking the cyst to the base of the tongue via the foramen cecum), and the presence of a separate, normal orthotopic thyroid gland below it.

Dermoid Cyst - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The mass elevating with tongue protrusion, whereas dermoid cysts, though midline, do not typically attach to the hyoid bone or base of the tongue.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a rapidly expanding, acutely infected midline neck mass. The immediate clinical necessity was to rule out a deep neck abscess causing airway compromise. Physical examination definitively localized the mass and utilized pathognomonic clinical tests (elevation with swallowing and tongue protrusion) to secure the diagnosis of an infected thyroglossal duct cyst. By confirming airway patency and definitively identifying the embryological remnant, the urgent visit prevented a traumatic emergency department admission, relieved parental anxiety of a malignant thyroid tumor, and safely initiated high-dose empiric oral antibiotics.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Amoxicillin/Clavulanate 875/125mg BD for 7 days to aggressively treat the acute bacterial superinfection. Advised oral Paracetamol 1g and Ibuprofen 400mg TDS for analgesia and anti-inflammatory effect. Generated an urgent outpatient referral for an ultrasound of the neck to confirm the cyst and verify the presence of normal thyroid tissue.
Positive Behavioral Change: To encourage spontaneous drainage if a sinus tract begins to form, and to manage the severe localized pain, the patient must apply a warm, moist compress to the midline neck for 15 minutes four times daily. The immediate, soothing relief of the heat provides positive reinforcement, ensuring compliance with the localized therapy required to supplement systemic antibiotics and prevent further abscess expansion.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48-72 hours. Subsequent referral to ENT for Sistrunk procedure (surgical excision) is required once the acute infection completely resolves.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops noisy breathing (stridor), difficulty catching their breath, absolute inability to swallow saliva, or if the redness spreads rapidly down the chest.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; requires ultrasound confirmation to ensure it is not functioning ectopic thyroid tissue.
Clinical Course: The acute redness, swelling, and pain should subside within 3-5 days on antibiotics. The lump itself will likely remain and will require surgical removal later to prevent it from ever infecting again.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your breathing becomes noisy or difficult, or if you feel like your airway is closing, you must call 000 immediately as the swelling can push backwards.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A tiny tube left over from before you were born (when your thyroid gland was forming) has filled with fluid and become acutely infected. The antibiotics will kill the bacteria. The lump moves when you stick your tongue out because the tube is still attached to the base of your tongue.
Actionable Steps: Take the antibiotics strictly as prescribed. Apply warm compresses to the area. Do not attempt to pop or squeeze the lump, as it can cause the infection to track deeper into your neck.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Infected Thyroglossal Cyst (Neck Cyst). Start oral antibiotics immediately. Apply warm compresses 4 times daily. If breathing becomes noisy or difficult, go straight to ED.

SMS EDUCATION

Do not squeeze or press the lump. Take your antibiotics with food. You will need an ultrasound and a GP review in 3 days to arrange a specialist referral for its eventual removal.
